## Solution

**The element.** Take $g$ to be rotation by $\pi$ about the $z$-axis:

$$g = \begin{pmatrix} -1 & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix} \in SO(3).$$

---

**Computing the centralizer.** We seek all $h \in SO(3)$ with $hg = gh$. Write

$$h = \begin{pmatrix} a & b & c \\ d & e & f \\ p & q & r \end{pmatrix}.$$

Then

$$hg = \begin{pmatrix} -a & -b & c \\ -d & -e & f \\ -p & -q & r \end{pmatrix}, \qquad gh = \begin{pmatrix} -a & -b & -c \\ -d & -e & -f \\ p & q & r \end{pmatrix}.$$

Equating entries forces $c = f = p = q = 0$. Hence every commuting $h$ is block-diagonal:

$$h = \begin{pmatrix} A & 0 \\ 0 & r \end{pmatrix}, \qquad A \in \mathbb{R}^{2\times 2},\; r \in \mathbb{R}.$$

Since $h \in SO(3)$: orthogonality gives $r = \pm 1$ and $A \in O(2)$; the determinant condition $\det h = \det(A)\cdot r = 1$ gives $\det(A) = r$.

- **Case $r = +1$:** $A \in SO(2)$, so $A = \begin{pmatrix} \cos\phi & -\sin\phi \\ \sin\phi & \cos\phi \end{pmatrix}$. These are rotations about the $z$-axis, forming a subgroup $H_0 \cong SO(2)$.

- **Case $r = -1$:** $\det(A) = -1$, so $A \in O(2)\setminus SO(2)$, e.g. $A = \begin{pmatrix} \cos\phi & \sin\phi \\ \sin\phi & -\cos\phi \end{pmatrix}$. These are rotations by $\pi$ about axes lying in the $xy$-plane. This set $H_1$ is a coset of $H_0$, not a subgroup.

Therefore

$$Z(g) = H_0 \;\sqcup\; H_1 \;\cong\; O(2).$$

---

**Verifying the required properties.**

1. **Disconnected.** $Z(g) \cong O(2)$ has two connected components: $H_0 \cong SO(2)$ (the identity component) and $H_1$ (the other component, consisting of orientation-reversing elements of $O(2)$). These are separated by the continuous invariant $\det(A) \in \{+1,-1\}$, so $Z(g)$ is disconnected.

2. **Identity component is $SO(2)$.** The identity component $H_0$ is exactly the set of rotations about the $z$-axis, which is isomorphic to $SO(2)$ (parametrized by $\phi \in [0,2\pi)$ via $\phi \mapsto R_z(\phi)$).

---

**Why $\theta = \pi$ is special.** For a rotation by angle $\theta \neq 0,\pi$ about axis $\hat n$, a commuting rotation $h$ must satisfy $h\hat n = \hat n$ (since $hgh^{-1}$ is rotation by $\theta$ about $h\hat n$, and equality with $g$ requires $h\hat n = \hat n$ when $\theta \neq -\theta$). This forces $h$ to be a rotation about $\hat n$, giving $Z(g) \cong SO(2)$, which is connected. Only when $\theta = \pi$ does the alternative $h\hat n = -\hat n$ also work (since rotation by $\pi$ about $\hat n$ equals rotation by $\pi$ about $-\hat n$), enlarging the centralizer to $O(2)$ and making it disconnected.

### PROOF COMPLETE
